<speak>
<p>Witness. Field Season One. Chapter Six: The Tree That Outlived the Dinosaurs. The Wollemi pine.</p>
<break time="800ms"/>
<p>This chapter's subject cannot run, hide, call, or care that you are listening. It is a tree — the first plant in this Field Season — and it may be the oldest story any living thing on Earth can tell. About forty-five mature Wollemi pines grow wild today, in four small stands at the bottom of one canyon system in Australia's Blue Mountains. Their pollen is indistinguishable from fossil pollen ninety million years old. Their lineage watched the dinosaurs leave.</p>
<p>And here is the one thing you must know before anything else: nobody will tell you where they are. Not us. Not the botanists. Not the government of New South Wales. The secret is not a tease. It is the species' strongest shield — and this chapter is partly about why.</p>
<break time="1s"/>
<p>The past. Ninety million years, then a rope.</p>
<break time="600ms"/>
<p>The Wollemi pine's family flourished across the ancient southern supercontinent. Then Australia dried out, epoch after epoch, and the rainforest lineages retreated to the last damp, fire-sheltered places — until, about two million years ago, the tree's pollen vanishes from the fossil record entirely. Science reasonably filed it as extinct.</p>
<p>On the tenth of September, 1994, a national parks ranger named David Noble rappelled into a remote canyon on his day off, and walked into a grove of trees he could not name. Botanists could — barely. It was a new genus, alive out of deep time. The director of Sydney's botanic gardens called it the equivalent of finding a small dinosaur alive on Earth.</p>
<p>Fame nearly finished what the ice ages could not. Within a decade, trespassers had found the site — and on their boots came a root-rot disease, confirmed in the largest stand in 2005. The only known infection of the wild grove was carried in, almost certainly, by uninvited admirers. That is why the location is secret. And why even loving this tree from a distance is a form of protection.</p>
<break time="1s"/>
<p>The present. The fire, the rescue, the insurance policy.</p>
<break time="600ms"/>
<p>In the Black Summer of 2019 and 2020, a mega-fire burned about ninety percent of the national park, and it came for the canyon. What followed became famous: a covert operation, kept quiet to protect the site, in which air tankers laid fire retardant along the canyon rim, while specialist firefighters were winched from helicopters into the gorge to run irrigation lines and pump water, day after day, under a sky full of smoke. The groves survived. Of forty-nine large trees, only four escaped scorching. But the canopy held. And the canopy is what saved them.</p>
<p>The counts since then are almost absurdly intimate: forty-five mature trees, forty-six wild seedlings — and with a tree that sprouts multiple trunks from one root, even the word individual is a judgment call. Genetically, the news is stranger still. Studies across hundreds of markers found no detectable diversity at all. The wild Wollemi pines are, in effect, one plant. Copied.</p>
<p>So its people built the broadest insurance policy in conservation. Since 2006, anyone has been able to legally buy a cultivated Wollemi pine, and citizen scientists have recorded them growing in twenty-seven countries. More than two hundred and ten young trees went out to botanic gardens on three continents. And over eight hundred and thirty-five saplings now grow at new secret sites inside the park.</p>
<p>What is known: the wild population is counted nearly tree by tree; the threats — a pathogen, a fire, a footprint — are precisely identified; and the species now also lives in thousands of gardens that can never all burn at once.</p>
<p>What is unknown: whether the burnt juveniles recover. Whether the translocated groves — planted, burned within months, replanted — will ever be self-sustaining. And whether a species with essentially one genome can adapt to a changing climate at all.</p>
<break time="1s"/>
<p>The future. What would improve its odds.</p>
<break time="600ms"/>
<p>One. More hidden groves — in more canyons, chosen by soil and fire shelter, so no single catastrophe can be the last one.</p>
<p>Two. Clean boots, closed doors. Strict access control and decontamination are the whole defense against the root-rot disease. It has no cure in the wild. Only prevention.</p>
<p>Three. Buy the tree; never seek it. Every legally cultivated Wollemi pine funds conservation and thickens the insurance population. The public's greatest gift to this species is enthusiastic distance.</p>
<p>Four. Guard the recovery time. The groves survived one fire age. What they need is enough quiet years between fires to grow back.</p>
<p>A tree that stood among polar dinosaurs is now guarded like a state secret, watered by helicopter, and planted in backyards from Sydney to Scotland. Ninety million years on, its fate has become a collaboration.</p>
<break time="1s"/>
<p>One question to carry with you. The kindest thing anyone can do for this species is not visit it. Is there something — a place, a person, a wild thing — that you could love better by leaving alone?</p>
<break time="1s"/>
<p>Every claim in this chapter is backed by a dated source, listed in the chapter text. This narration uses a synthetic voice. Thank you for witnessing.</p>
</speak>
